(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_movingchargesandmagnetism=[{id:"neet-2026-q011",pn:11,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Force between parallel current-carrying wires",q:`Two infinitely long parallel conducting wires A and B carry currents I and 2I, respectively, in the same direction. The wire A has uniform mass per unit length \u03BB and lies on an insulated floor. The wire B is kept fixed at a height h above the floor. The minimum magnitude of h so that the wire A does not rise from the floor is:
[g is the acceleration due to gravity and \u03BC\u2080 is the permeability of free space.]`,o:["\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(\u03C0\u03BBg)","2\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(\u03C0\u03BBg)","4\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(\u03C0\u03BBg)","\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(2\u03C0\u03BBg)"],c:0,s:`Force per unit length on wire A by wire B: F = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)(2I\xB72I/h) = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)(4I\xB2/h), as written by the publisher.
If F_net \u2264 mg (weight of wire A per unit length is \u03BBg), the wire does not rise from the floor.
So F \u2264 \u03BBg.
(\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)(4I\xB2/h) \u2264 \u03BBg.
Therefore h \u2265 \u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(\u03C0\u03BBg).
Note: Publisher writes force as (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)(2I\xB72I)/h, which is not the product of the currents I and 2I; it simplifies to \u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(\u03C0h) = \u03BC\u2080(I)(2I)/(2\u03C0h), same as the correct value, so answer (1) holds`,sfig:{w:285,h:120,alt:"Wire B above carries 2I to the right; wire A below carries I to the right with mass per unit length \u03BB; a double arrow marks the gap h.",d:[["t",2,24,"wire-B","s"],["l",50,20,260,20,""],["l",215,20,222,20,"a"],["t",205,14,"2I","m"],["t",2,97,"wire-A","s"],["l",50,93,260,93,""],["l",135,93,142,93,"a"],["t",138,108,"I","m"],["t",264,97,"\u03BB",""],["l",95,20,95,93,"b"],["t",101,60,"h",""]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Field at centre of circular arc",q:"A current I\u2080 flows through a metallic circular loop of radius r as shown in the figure. Resistance of the segment ABC is half that of ADC. Magnitude of magnetic field at the centre O of the loop is :",o:["\u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(4r)","\u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(2r)","\u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(2\u03C0r)","\u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(12r)"],c:3,s:`The current I\u2080 enters at A and splits into I\u2081 in segment ABC and I\u2082 in segment ADC.
Both segments are in parallel, so I\u2081R_ABC = I\u2082R_ADC.
R_ABC = R/2, so I\u2081(R/2) = I\u2082R, which gives I\u2081/2 = I\u2082.
Also I\u2081 + I\u2082 = I\u2080, so 2I\u2082 + I\u2082 = I\u2080.
I\u2082 = I\u2080/3 and I\u2081 = 2I\u2080/3.
Each semicircle gives a field \u03BC\u2080I/(4r) at O, but the two fields point in opposite directions (one out of the page, one into the page).
B\u2080 = B\u2081 + B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081/(4r) (out) + \u03BC\u2080I\u2082/(4r) (in)
B\u2080 = \u03BC\u2080\xB72I\u2080/(4r\xD73) \u2212 \u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(4r\xD73) = \u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(12r).`,fig:{w:240,h:200,alt:"Circular loop of radius r with centre O; current I0 enters at A at the top and leaves at C at the bottom; left arc ABC is drawn thick and a dashed radius r points to the lower right.",d:[["c",120,100,60],["p","M120 41 A59 59 0 0 0 120 159","B"],["p","M120 40 A60 60 0 0 0 120 160","B"],["p","M120 39 A61 61 0 0 0 120 161","B"],["l",120,30,120,170,"d"],["l",120,8,120,36,"a"],["l",120,164,120,192,"a"],["t",130,18,"I\u2080"],["t",130,184,"I\u2080"],["t",107,56,"A","m"],["t",107,148,"C","m"],["t",66,104,"B"],["t",162,104,"D"],["c",120,100,2,"F"],["t",104,112,"O"],["l",120,100,162,142,"da"],["t",150,118,"r"]]},sfig:{w:240,h:210,alt:"Loop with current I\u2080 entering at A and leaving at C; the current splits into I\u2081 in the left arc ABC and I\u2082 in the right arc ADC.",d:[["c",120,105,55],["l",120,50,120,160,"d"],["l",120,12,120,48,"a"],["t",128,26,"I\u2080"],["l",120,162,120,198,"a"],["t",128,190,"I\u2080"],["t",104,66,"A"],["t",104,154,"C"],["t",70,110,"B"],["c",120,105,2.5,"F"],["t",106,118,"O"],["t",160,110,"D"],["l",74,64,67,80,"a"],["t",50,66,"I\u2081"],["l",166,64,173,80,"a"],["t",176,66,"I\u2082"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q003",pn:3,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Velocity selector, crossed E and B",q:"An electron (mass 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg and charge 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C) moving with speed c/100 (c = speed of light) is injected into a magnetic field B\u20D7 of magnitude 9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T perpendicular to its direction of motion. We wish to apply an uniform electric field E\u20D7 together with the magnetic field so that the electron does not deflect from its path. Then (speed of light c = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 ms\u207B\xB9)",o:["E\u20D7 is perpendicular to B\u20D7 and its magnitude is 27 \xD7 10\u2074 V m\u207B\xB9","E\u20D7 is perpendicular to B\u20D7 and its magnitude is 27 \xD7 10\xB2 V m\u207B\xB9","E\u20D7 is parallel to B\u20D7 and its magnitude is 27 \xD7 10\xB2 V m\u207B\xB9","E\u20D7 is parallel to B\u20D7 and its magnitude is 27 \xD7 10\u2074 V m\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`For no deflection of the electron, F_B = F_E.
\u2212e(v\u20D7 \xD7 B\u20D7) = \u2212eE\u20D7
\u21D2 E\u20D7 = v\u20D7 \xD7 B\u20D7 \u21D2 E\u20D7 \u22A5 B\u20D7
E = vB = (c/100) \xD7 9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074
= (3 \xD7 10\u2078/100) \xD7 9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074
= 27 \xD7 10\xB2 V m\u207B\xB9`,sfig:{w:190,h:210,alt:"An electron moving right with velocity v; the force F_E on it points up and F_B points down; B points into the page (two crossed circles) and E points down.",d:[["c",25.6,83.2,16,""],["t",25.6,87.2,"e\u207B","m"],["l",41.6,83.2,104,83.2,"da"],["t",110.4,87.2,"v",""],["l",25.6,67.2,25.6,20,"a"],["t",21.6,13.6,"F",""],["t",28.8,16.8,"E","s"],["l",25.6,99.2,25.6,145.6,"a"],["t",21.6,161.6,"F",""],["t",28.8,164.8,"B","s"],["c",78.4,28,8,""],["l",73.6,23.2,83.2,32.8,""],["l",83.2,23.2,73.6,32.8,""],["c",101.6,28,8,""],["l",96.8,23.2,106.4,32.8,""],["l",106.4,23.2,96.8,32.8,""],["t",125.6,33.6,"B",""],["t",125.6,24,"\u2192","s"],["l",146.4,86.4,146.4,132,"a"],["l",169.6,86.4,169.6,132,"a"],["t",152.8,153.6,"E",""],["t",152.8,144,"\u2192","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q012",pn:12,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Magnetic moment of a current loop",q:"A 2 amp current is flowing through two different small circular copper coils having radii ratio 1 : 2. The ratio of their respective magnetic moments will be",o:["1 : 4","1 : 2","2 : 1","4 : 1"],c:0,s:`Magnetic moment of a current carrying circular loop = IA
M = IA
M \u221D A [I is the same]
M\u2081/M\u2082 = A\u2081/A\u2082 = \u03C0r\u2081\xB2/\u03C0r\u2082\xB2 = (1/2)\xB2 = 1/4`},{id:"neet-2025-q018",pn:18,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Magnetic moment of orbiting electron",q:"A model for quantized motion of an electron in a uniform magnetic field B states that the flux passing through the orbit of the electron is n(h/e) where n is an integer, h is Planck\u2019s constant and e is the magnitude of electron\u2019s charge. According to the model, the magnetic moment of an electron in its lowest energy state will be (m is the mass of the electron)",o:["he/(\u03C0m)","he/(2\u03C0m)","heB/(\u03C0m)","heB/(2\u03C0m)"],c:1,s:`Magnetic force provides the centripetal force: evB = mv\xB2/r
v = eBr/m
Flux \u03C6 = BA, so nh/e = B\u03C0r\xB2
Br\xB2 = nh/(e\u03C0)
\u03BC = IA = (e/T)\u03C0r\xB2 = (e \xD7 v/(2\u03C0r))\u03C0r\xB2
\u03BC = evr/2 = (1/2) e \xD7 (eBr/m) \xD7 r
\u03BC = (1/2)e\xB2 (Br\xB2/m) = (1/2)e\xB2 (nh/(e\u03C0m))
\u03BC = neh/(2\u03C0m)
For n = 1 (lowest state): \u03BC = eh/(2\u03C0m)`}]})();
